WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.000
I'm not saying this is definitely a plot

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.880
for a British Ocean's-11 style
heist caper,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.200
but if there are any screenwriters
out there watching this:

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.360
I feel like this one might be for you.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.320
British coins have the year that they
were manufactured right on them.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.520
So if you're a coin collector,
and your dream is the full set,

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.080
it's not just a case of finding
one of each design:

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.040
you also want each value
from each year.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.480
But in 1933, Britain didn't
need any more pennies.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.760
There were plenty in circulation,

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.560
so the Royal Mint,
who make - or "mint" - coins,

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.920
decided they wouldn't make
any one-pence coins that year.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.920
That's not unusual,
it had happened before.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.480
Trouble is, King George V needed some.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.560
It was tradition for the King to bury
a full set of new coins from that year

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.840
in the foundations of new and important
buildings as they were constructed,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.560
and you couldn't use old coins for that.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.480
So the Royal Mint did make
some 1933 pennies.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.680
No-one actually kept the records

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.055
of how many there were,
but it was probably...

00:00:54.055 --> 00:00:55.120
seven.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.120
Which means that while the face value
of those seven coins is a penny,

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.760
they're so rare that the price
at auction these days

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.960
is easily more than £100,000.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.280
And over at a London coin dealership,
I got to see one of them.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.320
- This is from a private collection,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.800
one that's been loaned to us very kindly.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.520
It's great because it's such an iconic coin,

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.760
we get a lot of visitors coming to see it.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.600
Usually these coins disappear into safes
and they're never seen again.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.560
The last sold in 2016 for a hammer price
of around £140,000.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.080
It's the first time one's been sold
for many, many years

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.200
and one would imagine, if it was
to come on the market again,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.800
it would sell for even higher.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.240
- So there are probably seven 1933 pennies.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.640
Three are in private collections
I got to film one of those.
 &nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.440
Two are in museums.
And the others...
 &nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.760
...well, that's the possible heist movie.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.600
Because remember, the king
buried some of the coins.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.960
One of them was buried under this church
in Middleton, West Yorkshire.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.320
I say "was" because in 1970,
it was stolen.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.160
Whereabouts still unknown,
although presumably

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.680
if someone in Yorkshire finds a 1933 penny
in their grandparents' coin collection,

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.480
there'll be a bit of suspicion.
And after this theft...

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.960
...the local bishop ordered that
the coins under this church

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.320
should be dug up and sold

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.360
in order to prevent them
falling into the hands of thieves.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.560
That's one of the pennies that's
in private hands legally now.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.960
Which means there is one building
and one buried coin left.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.920
The last 1933 penny is buried somewhere
under the foundations of Senate House,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.360
at the University of London.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.120
Or at least, everyone thinks it is.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.600
No-one's checked since 1933,
you'd have to dig it up.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.640
And I doubt anyone actually knows
exactly where it's buried,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.680
unless they've done a huge amount of research
and found some original blueprints.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.800
Which, to be clear, I haven't.
And besides, according to the university,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.920
the whole package is buried very deep,
not exactly where you'd expect,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.760
which does sound like
what someone would write

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.720
if they were trying to discourage
amateur treasure-hunting thieves.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.360
But Senate House is right here,
in the middle of London.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.120
There is some construction around here today,

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.760
a few people in high-viz jackets
with power tools.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.880
And while it's not exactly
cracking the vault of a Las Vegas casino...

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.840
well, I don't have a plan for you.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.240
And I don't recommend anyone try it for real.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.720
But it is a heist movie waiting to happen.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.360
Seriously, don't try it. This isn't the movies.
It's... it's just a coin.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.840
I don't have any plans to...

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.742
How do I make this sound not sarcastic?

